{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>All [the words] that HaShem has spoken — we will do.</b> They did not immediately say 'we will do and we will hear.' It is possible to explain according to the opinion of the Sages of Sepharad, who explained that this entire pericope was after the receiving of the Ten Commandments — if so, it is just that they said at first 'we will do,' for they thought that HaShem would not command them more than these Ten Commandments. However, afterward, when they saw that Moses took half the blood and put it in basins and half the blood he threw upon the altar, then this thought arose in their mind: the second half — where will he put it? Surely the intention is that now He has given us only half the Torah and the commandments, and He will yet add to us commandment after commandment, and at that time he will throw the second half upon the altar. Therefore they said: 'All that HaShem has spoken — we will do and we will hear.' And Moses understood their thought and said: You are mistaken, for the dividing of the blood is in order to throw the second half upon the people, to signify that through the receiving of the Torah they would become as one, in love and affection with HaShem, blessed be He. And also according to Rashi's explanation, who explained that this pericope was before the Ten Commandments, we may say that at the first time they heard the seven commandments of the children of Noah, and Sabbath, and civil laws, and honoring father and mother, and the red heifer, as Rashi explained, and in the manner that has been expounded."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:34.224345388Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}